From 5e345497643148794fb41105e270c9864b5956a0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 00:42:31 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08036/11362] The induce budget is not the lever;
 repeat_penalty is, and it was never set

Phase 1 asked whether ft09's induction is budget-starved. It is not, and chasing that
question turned up two things that matter more.

BUDGET: NO. Measured live on gemma-4-31B-it against ft09's REAL 4343-token induction prompt
-- 4096/8192/16384 x 3 seeded attempts on the combined and the engine-only induce call, and
on the refactor call that actually truncated in the episode -- zero usable engines in every
arm. The 4096 arm reproduces the live failure exactly (combined rejected 3/3, engine-only
accepted 3/3 and non-returning 3/3), and both acceptance gates pass, including "the budget
axis is uncensored": every capped call reported predicted_n equal to its own budget, never
the short-of-budget signature of a shared-pool truncation. The lever was fairly tested.

Why it cannot work: the generator is in a repetition loop, not short of room. At matched seed
and temperature, doubling the budget leaves the set of DISTINCT emitted lines unchanged
(46 -> 46, 118 -> 118 on two of three engine seeds) while the emitted length doubles. On the
combined call, mean code lines are 20.3 at 4096, 8192 AND 16384 while mean predicted_n goes
3813 -> 6544 -> 12005. One 16384 completion is 5345 non-blank lines drawn from 24 distinct
ones. This reproduces the finding already inline in induce() from 2026-06-25 ("a budget bump
does NOT help; the model just rambles more"), now on the current model and both call shapes.
The rerun was justified rather than assumed: REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-35 kept 4096 because the 8192
requirement "was specific to a 3x larger, non-live candidate"; the live generator BECAME that
class on 2026-07-28 and the default never moved.

NOT ONE GENERATED ENGINE EVER CHANGES THE GRID. Every completion was RUN against ft09's 25
real transitions. Across both induce shapes, the refactor call and every budget, zero engines
produce an output different from their input. The refactor completions look best on paper --
accepted, parsing, returning on every path, 19 of 25 heldout-EXACT -- and they are the
identity function. 19 of the 25 transitions are no-ops, so "nothing ever changes" gets every
one right; cell recall is 0.000. That is the vacuous pass the change-aware gate exists to
catch, reproduced from the generator side. It also reframes the question: the engines are not
incomplete, they are inert, and no budget fixes inert. It is why the headline metric here is
not "returns on every path" -- an identity engine clears that trivially, and one did.

REPEAT_PENALTY IS THE LEVER. Same prompt, seed, temperature, budget, server and card, one
flag apart:

  arm                    hit cap  accepted  returns  CHANGES  mean cell recall
  off (SHIPPED)              3/3       3/3      0/3      0/3            0.0000
  repeat_penalty 1.1         0/3       3/3      3/3      3/3            0.9474
  dry_multiplier 0.8         1/3       2/3      1/3      1/3            0.1053

The shipped configuration burns the whole budget and returns nothing. repeat_penalty 1.1
stops naturally at 1133-1912 tokens with ft09's real mechanic in it -- a 6x6 block at
(y-2, x-2) toggling between colours 8 and 9 -- on every attempt, and is the only arm in the
sweep producing a non-degenerate engine. The scored path runs with every repetition control
off (repeat_penalty 1.0, dry 0.0, frequency/presence 0.0, read from the server's own /props)
not by a tuned decision but because generate() never names the parameter.

NOT SHIPPED, deliberately. Changing how the scored agent samples is a behaviour change, not a
measurement change. Three caveats bound the result: the cell recall is IN-SAMPLE (all 6
grid-changing transitions appear in the prompt; the 16 held-out ones are every one a no-op,
so there are zero held-out changed cells); n=3 on one game and one prompt; and the two
controls are not interchangeable, so the recommendation names repeat_penalty, not "a
repetition penalty". The operator's next measurement is the same arm across the other five
games.

Two further findings. The seed does not reach across server instances: identical prompt, seed
and config on a second llama-server gives non-identical completions, while within one process
it holds byte-exactly across a 4x budget range -- a live explanation for how ft09's refactor
hit the cap 3/3 in the episode while the same prompt completes naturally in replay, and an
uncontrolled term in any A/B whose arms cross server processes. And ft09's banked engine was
mis-attributed: its halves tokenize to 4093 + 81 against a 4096 budget and it carries
_combine_world_model()'s injected prefix, so it came from the SPLIT fallback via the
post-refinement re-induce at arc_competition_agent.py:5889, whose message is discarded.

The prompts are the live agent's own, checked not assumed: the capture run's action trace is
element-by-element identical to the banked live LLM-ON cell, 60 of 60, sha fba0baa5d5473eac.

The arithmetic, computed from the repo's own constants: the SHIPPED default already does not
fit a 24 GiB card (81920 cells -> 23888 MiB + 1500 guard vs 24123 free), which is why every
local measurement pins CARNOT_ARC_INDUCE_N_CTX=32768. The largest pool a 24 GiB card holds at
L=0 is 53248. At the pinned 32768 and K=1 ft09 could have spent 28425 completion tokens; at
K=4 -- the eval framework's shape -- the same pool leaves it 3849, already below today's 4096.

A drift defect was found and fixed but NOT landed. _INDUCE_DEFAULT_MAX_TOKENS documents
itself as binding the budget at four sites; it had one reader and the other three were
independent hardcoded 4096 literals, so editing it in source grew the context pool and left
the budget alone. The fix, a mutation-verified regression test and REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-40 are
preserved at ops/pending-fixes/. artifact-freshness-lint marks 12 registered artifacts stale
on any edit to the two agentic modules, and discharging that needs 8 rebuilds -- one a LIVE
capture whose re-run would replace published numbers with fresh nondeterministic ones -- plus
4 acknowledgements. That price is the operator's call.

Banked ARC levels remain 3/3 and the submission gate is NOT met. The funnel is unchanged: the
LLM tier fires 6/6 and reaches the policy 1/6. This measures one game's induce calls in
isolation; only a re-run live cell could move that count, and none was run.

